## Build and validate

Build, copy the produced DLL beside `plugin.wsgm.json`, then:

```powershell
dotnet build {{ROOT_NAMESPACE}}.csproj -c Release -r win-x64
wsgm-device validate <package-directory>
wsgm-device test plugin <package-directory> --from <inventory.json>
wsgm-device pack <package-directory> --out <plugin.wsgmpkg>
```

The hardware action is separate and always attended:

```powershell
wsgm-device test hardware <package-directory> --from <inventory.json> --state-dir <new-directory> --action haptic
```

Use `--action controller`, or
`--action capability --capability <id> --value <semantic-value> [--instance <id>]`, for the other
attended actions.

## Installing

Close the WSGM shell first. Installing makes this directory trusted hardware code that may later
inherit WSGM's elevation, so inspect the exact files before you do it.

Install or replace the single protected plugin slot with the installed WSGM maintenance command. It
requests elevation and refuses to run while a machine-wide WSGM or Device Lab hardware owner is
active:

```powershell
& "$env:LOCALAPPDATA\WSGM\bin\WSGM.exe" --install-device-plugin <package-directory>
if ($LASTEXITCODE -ne 0) { throw 'WSGM rejected the plugin installation; inspect wsgm.log.' }
```

The package directory has to be separate from the protected installed slot. Pass exactly this
command and directory; maintenance flags cannot be combined with normal WSGM modes.

For the complete workflow and the safety contract, see `docs/device-plugin-authoring.md` in the WSGM
repository.
